  "worked_solution": "Applying the mean value theorem for integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c)(b-a) & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ f(c) & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3-(-3)}{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{x}^{2}+2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{x}^{2})|}_{-3}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[(\\frac{{3}^{3}}{3}+{3}^{2})-(\\frac{(-3)^{3}}{3}+(-3)^{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot [(9+9)-(-9+9)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the expression for $f(x)$ to solve for the value of $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(c) & =3 \\\\ {c}^{2}+2c & =3 \\\\ {c}^{2}+2c-3 & =0 \\\\ (c+1)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ c+1 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ c & =-3,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the values of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem on the interval $[-3,3]$ are \n\n$$\nc=-3,\\,\\text{and}\\,c=1.\n$$",
